  <main class="legal">
    <h1>API Reference</h1>
    <p class="updated mono">Integrate incidents, scanner results, and infrastructure data with your own tools</p>

    <p>The StdOut API lets you push incidents from external systems, pull your infrastructure data, and manage your account programmatically. All authenticated endpoints use Bearer token auth.</p>

    <h2>Authentication</h2>

    <p>Most API endpoints require a Bearer token. Generate tokens in <a href="/app/settings">Settings → API Tokens</a>. Each token is shown once — copy it before leaving the page.</p>

    <p>Pass the token in the <code>Authorization</code> header:</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN</code></pre>

    <p>Tokens are per-account and carry full read/write access. Treat them like passwords. You can generate multiple tokens (useful for different integrations) and revoke any of them individually without affecting others.</p>

    <h2>Base URL</h2>

    <p>All endpoints are relative to your StdOut instance:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>SaaS:</strong> <code>https://app.stdout.run</code></li>
      <li><strong>Self-hosted:</strong> your configured domain</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Incidents</h2>

    <h3>Create an incident</h3>

    <p>Push an incident from any external system — a monitoring script, a failed cron job, an uptime checker, a CI pipeline. Anything that can make an HTTP request can open an incident in StdOut.</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>POST /app/api/incidents/webhook
Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Backup job failed on nas01",
  "severity": "high",
  "source": "cron",
  "body": "Exit code 1. Destination /mnt/backup not mounted.",
  "tags": ["backup", "nas01"]
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Request fields</h3>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>title</code></td><td>string</td><td>Yes</td><td>Short description of the incident (appears in the dashboard list)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>severity</code></td><td>string</td><td>No</td><td><code>low</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code>high</code>, <code>critical</code>. Defaults to <code>medium</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>source</code></td><td>string</td><td>No</td><td>Where the incident originated — e.g. <code>cron</code>, <code>monitor</code>, <code>deploy</code>, <code>uptime-kuma</code>. Free text.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>body</code></td><td>string</td><td>No</td><td>Full error output, log excerpt, or additional context. Markdown supported.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tags</code></td><td>string[]</td><td>No</td><td>Labels for filtering and search.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Response</h3>

    <pre is:raw><code>HTTP 201 Created

{
  "id": "inc_01j...",
  "title": "Backup job failed on nas01",
  "severity": "high",
  "status": "open",
  "created_at": "2025-04-01T14:23:00Z"
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Example: bash script</h3>

    <p>A one-liner to open an incident from any shell script:</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>#!/bin/bash
# Run your command; open an incident if it fails

if ! /usr/local/bin/backup.sh; then
  curl -s -X POST https://app.stdout.run/app/api/incidents/webhook \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $STDOUT_TOKEN" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{
      "title": "Backup script failed",
      "severity": "high",
      "source": "cron",
      "body": "See /var/log/backup.log for details"
    }'
fi</code></pre>

    <h3>Example: n8n HTTP Request node</h3>

    <p>In n8n, add an HTTP Request node after any failing branch:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Method: <code>POST</code></li>
      <li>URL: <code>https://app.stdout.run/app/api/incidents/webhook</code></li>
      <li>Authentication: Header Auth → <code>Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN</code></li>
      <li>Body: JSON with <code>title</code>, <code>severity</code>, <code>source</code>, <code>body</code></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Scanner results</h2>

    <h3>Push a scan</h3>

    <p>The Scanner uses this endpoint to push discovery results. You can also push custom stack data directly if you have your own inventory source.</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>POST /app/api/stacks/import
Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "source": "scanner",
  "scan_type": "docker",
  "data": { ... }
}</code></pre>

    <p>The scanner binary handles this automatically — you don't need to call this manually unless you're building a custom integration.</p>

    <h2>Scanner schedule</h2>

    <h3>Get current schedule</h3>

    <pre is:raw><code>GET /app/api/scanner/schedule
Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN</code></pre>

    <pre is:raw><code>HTTP 200 OK

{
  "enabled": true,
  "frequency": "daily",
  "time": "03:00",
  "modules": ["docker", "metrics", "network"],
  "delta_only": true
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Update schedule</h3>

    <pre is:raw><code>PUT /app/api/scanner/schedule
Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "enabled": true,
  "frequency": "daily",
  "time": "03:00",
  "modules": ["docker", "metrics", "network", "dns"],
  "delta_only": true
}</code></pre>

    <p>A persistent scanner container polls this endpoint and applies changes without requiring a restart. Frequency options: <code>hourly</code>, <code>daily</code>, <code>weekly</code>.</p>

    <h2>Export</h2>

    <h3>Export all data</h3>

    <pre is:raw><code>GET /app/api/export
Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN</code></pre>

    <p>Returns a JSON archive of your full account — incidents, stacks, knowledge base docs, and preferences. Use this for backups or migrations.</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>HTTP 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "exported_at": "2025-04-01T14:23:00Z",
  "account": { ... },
  "incidents": [ ... ],
  "stacks": [ ... ],
  "docs": [ ... ]
}</code></pre>

    <h2>Token management</h2>

    <h3>List tokens</h3>

    <pre is:raw><code>GET /app/api/tokens
Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN (session cookie also accepted)</code></pre>

    <pre is:raw><code>HTTP 200 OK

{
  "tokens": [
    {
      "id": "tok_01j...",
      "name": "scanner",
      "created_at": "2025-03-15T09:00:00Z",
      "last_used_at": "2025-04-01T03:00:00Z"
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Create a token</h3>

    <pre is:raw><code>POST /app/api/tokens
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "my-script" }</code></pre>

    <p>The full token value is returned once in the response. It is not stored and cannot be retrieved again.</p>

    <h3>Revoke a token</h3>

    <pre is:raw><code>DELETE /app/api/tokens/:id</code></pre>

    <h2>Account</h2>

    <h3>Current user</h3>

    <pre is:raw><code>GET /app/api/me
Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN (session cookie also accepted)</code></pre>

    <pre is:raw><code>HTTP 200 OK

{
  "id": "usr_01j...",
  "email": "you@example.com",
  "plan": "solo",
  "workspaces": [
    { "id": "ws_01j...", "name": "homelab", "role": "owner" }
  ]
}</code></pre>

    <h2>Errors</h2>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>400</code></td><td>Bad request — missing required field or invalid value</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>401</code></td><td>Missing or invalid token</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>403</code></td><td>Token valid but action not permitted for your plan</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>404</code></td><td>Resource not found</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>429</code></td><td>Rate limited — back off and retry</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>500</code></td><td>Server error — check status or contact support</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>All error responses include a JSON body:</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>{
  "error": "missing required field: title"
}</code></pre>

  </main>

